#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "35be9962-fbfd-57f5-906e-41660079063d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5171" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x$. We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{3} - 6x^{2} + 4x &= 0 \\\\\n2x(x^{2} - 3x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x(x^{2} - 3x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n2x(x - 1)(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$, $x = 1$, and $x = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/id #uuid "ccefedb4-9e45-56c4-bb11-460ed8fe1793" :question/math-academy-id "e-5172" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $x^3 - x^2 - x = 3x^2 + 4x$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0$. To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - x^{2} - x &= 3x^{2} + 4x \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - x &= 4x \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - 5x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x$. We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 4x^{2} - 5x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4x - 5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} - 4x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$, $x = -1$, and $x = 5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "830e79ed-49ac-537f-aa67-9bfd063bb258" :question/math-academy-id "e-5173" :question/problem "If $3x^5 + x^3 = 3x^3 - 5x^4$, then what are the values of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{5} + x^{3} &= 3x^{3} - 5x^{4} \\\\\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} + x^{3} &= 3x^{3} \\\\\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} - 2x^{3} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x^3$. We can factor out the $x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{5} + 5x^{4} - 2x^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3}(3x^{2} + 5x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3x^2+5x-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3}(3x^{2} + 5x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3}(3x - 1)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0$,\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand $x = -2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4014c6b-68b5-5870-921c-cae228030bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 0, 4, \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070116} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb2700e-0e88-5026-bc6b-7c3d7a27c9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z =-4, 0, \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070117}